<p class="gna-lede">The cost of jaw surgery in Hyderabad depends on which operation you need, where it is done, what it involves and how much of it your insurance will cover. The main drivers are single versus double jaw surgery, the hospital and room category, plates and 3D planning, orthodontic treatment, and the length of your stay. A reliable figure only comes from a written, itemised estimate prepared after an examination and planning.</p>

<h2 id="why-no-single-price">Why there is no single price for jaw surgery</h2>
<p>"Jaw surgery" covers many different operations: correcting a misaligned bite (orthognathic surgery), repairing a fracture, treating the jaw joint, or removing a cyst or tumour. Even within corrective surgery, one person may need a single movement of the lower jaw while another needs both jaws moved, the chin reshaped and wisdom teeth removed. This guide focuses on corrective (orthognathic) surgery, but the same factors apply to most planned jaw operations.</p>
<p>We do not publish package prices, because a figure without a diagnosis is likely to mislead. What we can do is explain what drives the cost, so you know what to ask and how to compare estimates fairly.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The type and number of procedures is the biggest single factor in the cost.</li>
    <li>Hospital tariffs and the room category you choose can change the hospital bill considerably, and can affect insurance payouts.</li>
    <li>Orthodontic treatment is usually charged separately and is rarely covered by insurance.</li>
    <li>Insurance may cover surgery done for a functional or medical reason, subject to policy terms and pre-authorisation; purely cosmetic surgery is usually excluded.</li>
    <li>Always compare itemised estimates for the same planned treatment, not headline figures.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="type-of-procedure">Type of procedure: single jaw, double jaw and add-ons</h2>
<p>The operation itself sets the length of time in theatre, the amount of hardware used and the likely hospital stay. The main options are:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Lower jaw surgery</strong> (bilateral sagittal split osteotomy), to move the lower jaw forward or back.</li>
  <li><strong>Upper jaw surgery</strong> (Le Fort I osteotomy), to move the upper jaw forward, up, down or to correct a tilt.</li>
  <li><strong>Double jaw (bimaxillary) surgery</strong>, moving both jaws in one operation. This takes longer and uses more plates and screws.</li>
  <li><strong>Genioplasty</strong>, reshaping or repositioning the chin, either on its own or added to jaw surgery.</li>
  <li><strong>Additional procedures</strong>, such as removing wisdom teeth, surgically assisted widening of the upper jaw, or nasal work done at the same sitting.</li>
</ul>
<p>Your own health matters too. If you have diabetes, high blood pressure, a heart condition or a bleeding tendency, you may need extra tests, a physician's review or closer monitoring after surgery, all of which add to the bill. Our <a href="/orthognathic-surgery">orthognathic surgery page</a> describes each procedure in more detail.</p>

<h2 id="planning-and-hardware">Scans, 3D planning and fixation plates</h2>
<p>Before corrective surgery, the team needs accurate records. These typically include a cephalogram (side skull X-ray), a panoramic X-ray, a cone-beam CT or CT scan, digital scans of the teeth and photographs. Many surgeons now use <a href="/blogs/role-of-3d-virtual-planning/">3D virtual surgical planning</a>, in which the jaw movements are planned on a computer model and printed surgical guides or splints are made to transfer the plan to the operating table. Planning software, printing and laboratory work each carry a cost.</p>
<p>The bones are held in their new positions with titanium plates and screws. The number needed depends on the movements; different manufacturers' systems are priced differently; and patient-specific (custom-made) plates, which are designed from your scan, generally cost more than standard ones. Resorbable plates are used in some situations. Your estimate should list the type of fixation planned.</p>

<h2 id="hospital-and-stay">Hospital, room category and length of stay</h2>
<p>Surgery at Gnathos Facial is carried out at NABH-accredited partner hospitals in Hyderabad, including CARE Hospitals, KIMS, Apollo Spectra and Yashoda. Each hospital has its own tariff for theatre time, anaesthesia, nursing, medicines and consumables, so the same operation can cost different amounts in different hospitals.</p>
<p>Within a hospital, the <strong>room category</strong> (general ward, shared room, single room or suite) affects not only the room charge but often other charges linked to it. Many insurance policies set a limit on room rent; if you choose a room above that limit, some insurers reduce the payout on other parts of the bill in proportion. It is worth checking this clause before you are admitted.</p>
<p><strong>Length of stay</strong> also matters. Most people stay one to three nights after corrective jaw surgery, sometimes with the first night in a high-dependency area for closer monitoring. A longer stay may be needed after double jaw surgery, if swelling or nausea makes drinking difficult, or if you have other medical conditions.</p>

<h2 id="orthodontics">Orthodontic treatment before and after surgery</h2>
<p>Most corrective jaw surgery is done together with orthodontic treatment. Braces are usually worn for a period before surgery to position the teeth over the bone, and for a shorter period afterwards to fine-tune the bite. This often adds up to 18 months to 3 years in total, followed by retainers.</p>
<p>Orthodontic fees are usually charged separately by the orthodontist and depend on the type of appliance (metal braces, ceramic braces or clear aligners), the complexity of the tooth movements and the number of visits. Because orthodontics is out-patient dental treatment, it is rarely covered by health insurance, so plan for it separately.</p>

