<h2 id="insurance-cover">Does health insurance cover jaw surgery?</h2>
<p>Sometimes, and the reason for surgery is the key. Insurers look at whether the operation treats a medical or functional problem, or whether it is done mainly to change appearance.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>More likely to be considered:</strong> jaw surgery to correct a significant bite problem that affects chewing, speech or breathing; jaw advancement for obstructive sleep apnoea confirmed on a sleep study (see <a href="/blogs/can-jaw-surgery-help-with-sleep-apnea/">can jaw surgery help with sleep apnoea?</a>); correction of deformity after an injury; jaw-joint disease; and reconstruction after tumour or cancer surgery.</li>
  <li><strong>Usually excluded:</strong> surgery done purely for cosmetic reasons, and out-patient dental treatment such as braces.</li>
</ul>
<p>Even when surgery is medically indicated, policies differ. Check for waiting periods (including for pre-existing conditions), room-rent limits, co-payment clauses, sub-limits on specific procedures, and items such as certain consumables that the policy does not pay for. Group policies through employers may have different terms from individual policies.</p>
<p>Your documents must describe your condition honestly. We record the clinical findings, measurements and functional problems as they are; we cannot describe cosmetic surgery as medical to obtain cover, and doing so would put your policy at risk.</p>

<h2 id="pre-authorisation">How cashless pre-authorisation works</h2>
<p>For planned surgery at a network hospital, the usual steps are:</p>
<ol>
  <li>After your consultation and planning, the surgeon prepares a clinical summary describing the diagnosis, the functional problem and the proposed treatment.</li>
  <li>The hospital's insurance desk submits a pre-authorisation request with an estimate to your insurer or third-party administrator (TPA), usually several days before surgery.</li>
  <li>The insurer may approve an amount, ask for more information (such as X-rays, a sleep study report or photographs), or decline.</li>
  <li>At discharge, the hospital sends the final bill; the insurer settles the approved portion and you pay any difference, non-payable items or co-payment.</li>
</ol>
<p>If your hospital is outside your insurer's network, you may be able to pay first and claim reimbursement later, which needs original bills and reports. Our team can help you understand the paperwork, but the decision on cover always rests with your insurer.</p>

<h2 id="emi-and-payment">Paying in instalments</h2>
<p>Some patients spread the cost using EMI facilities from banks, credit-card issuers or healthcare finance companies, or through payment arrangements available at the treating hospital. If you are considering this, ask about the interest rate, processing fees, tenure and what happens if a payment is missed, and read the agreement before signing. Orthodontic fees are often paid in stages over the course of treatment, which also spreads the cost.</p>

<h2 id="getting-an-estimate">How to get a reliable estimate</h2>
<p>A trustworthy estimate comes after, not before, a proper assessment. The usual sequence is a consultation with the surgeon, an orthodontic opinion, the necessary scans, a treatment plan, and then a written estimate from the hospital billing desk that lists each component. Before you agree, ask:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Which procedures, hospital and room category does this estimate assume?</li>
  <li>Does it include the surgeon, anaesthetist, theatre, plates, planning, medicines and follow-up visits?</li>
  <li>What could increase the bill, such as a longer stay or an additional procedure?</li>
  <li>What will my insurer likely cover, and what will I pay myself?</li>
</ul>
<p>Remember that the cheapest estimate is not always the most complete. Jaw surgery is a significant operation with real risks, including swelling, numbness, infection and relapse, and occasionally further procedures such as plate removal. Recovery and results vary between individuals, so choose a team that explains the plan, the risks and the follow-up clearly. For some people, orthodontic treatment alone is a reasonable alternative; our guide on <a href="/blogs/underbite-correction-braces-or-jaw-surgery/">braces or jaw surgery for an underbite</a> explains how that decision is made. When you are ready, you can <a href="/book-appointment">book a consultation</a> in Gachibowli.</p>
